#
# The ext/ boundary, measured from dash's side.
#
# docs/spec/shell-matrix.md measured dash as the sole holdout on 13 of 21
# rows and drew the core there. ext/ proves the other three shells agree
# about those rows; nothing until now asked what dash *does* when it meets
# one, and "dash does not run ext/" was an exemption written in a Dirs list
# rather than a measurement.
#
# This is that measurement. Every construct ext/ has a file for is put to
# dash here and the refusal is recorded — which error, at which status. An
# exemption cannot regress; a recorded refusal can, and that is the whole
# difference.
#
# Each is inside `( eval … )`: a parse error in dash is fatal to the shell it
# is in, so the subshell is what lets the file reach its second line, and
# eval is what keeps the refusal at run time instead of taking the whole file
# out before anything runs.

# Arrays. ext/arrays.tests.
( eval 'a=(one two)' ) 2>&1
printf 'array: %s\n' "$?"

# The condition command. ext/dblbracket.tests.
( eval '[[ a == a ]]' ) 2>&1
printf 'double bracket: %s\n' "$?"

# Exponentiation, which is the arithmetic row dash is the holdout on.
( eval 'echo $((2**3))' ) 2>&1
printf 'power: %s\n' "$?"

# Pattern substitution. ext/patsub.tests.
( eval 'v=x; echo ${v/x/y}' ) 2>&1
printf 'pattern substitution: %s\n' "$?"

# Substrings. ext/substrings.tests.
( eval 'v=abc; echo ${v:1:1}' ) 2>&1
printf 'substring: %s\n' "$?"

# The `function` keyword. ext/funcform.tests.
( eval 'function f { echo hi; }' ) 2>&1
printf 'function keyword: %s\n' "$?"

# The C-style for loop. ext/cfor.tests.
( eval 'for ((i=0;i<1;i++)); do echo c; done' ) 2>&1
printf 'C-style for: %s\n' "$?"

# Here strings. ext/herestring.tests.
( eval 'cat <<< word' ) 2>&1
printf 'here string: %s\n' "$?"

# select. ext/select.tests.
( eval 'select x in a; do break; done < /dev/null' ) 2>&1
printf 'select: %s\n' "$?"

# Process substitution. ext/procsub.tests.
( eval 'cat <(echo p)' ) 2>&1
printf 'process substitution: %s\n' "$?"

# `+=` is the one row where dash does not refuse: there is no append
# operator, so `v+=y` is a command word and the shell goes looking for a
# program called `v+=y`. A refusal and a misreading are different findings
# and this is the second kind.
( eval 'v=x; v+=y; echo $v' ) 2>&1
printf 'append: %s\n' "$?"

# $'…' is the second misreading and the more dangerous one. ext/
# dollarquote.tests is a whole file of escapes; this dash reads the dollar as
# an ordinary character and the rest as a single-quoted string, so the word
# arrives with a literal dollar on the front and the escapes unprocessed, at
# status 0 and with nothing on standard error. A script that used it gets a
# wrong answer silently, which is exactly the class the corpus cannot see and
# a suite can.
( eval "printf '%s\n' \$'a\tb'" ) 2>&1
printf 'dollar quote: %s\n' "$?"
